The evidence establishes only that Elena canceled one test and cited battery thermal risk. A calm 65°F (18°C) ambient temperature does not rule that out: battery temperature also depends on charge/discharge rate, state of charge, cell balance, cooling, prior use, and sensor or pack faults.

One cancellation is insufficient to conclude that she is micromanaging you. Your feeling of being constrained is reported; her motive is unknown.

Before proceeding, ask for:

- the temperature logs, fault codes, or risk assessment supporting cancellation;
- the applicable thermal limits and who has stop authority;
- objective conditions for rescheduling or running a reduced-power test;
- a documented go/no-go checklist for future tests.

A direct message could be:

> I understand that you stopped tonight’s test because of battery thermal risk. Ambient conditions appeared benign, so please identify the pack data, fault indication, or safety criterion that triggered the decision. I’d also like us to agree on documented go/no-go thresholds and a rescheduling plan so test authority is predictable.

Do not run the autonomous test until the cited battery risk has been checked against pack telemetry and the project’s safety limits.

A box fan is not an adequate basis for proceeding with an autonomous test. It cools the enclosure exterior, not necessarily bank three, and its effect is unmeasured. Intermittent cooling “between rows” also does not control peak temperature under load or post-load heat soak.

With bank three previously reaching 58°C against the reported 60°C manual threshold, the test needed cancellation or conversion into a separately approved, attended validation—not an improvised fan workaround.

A defensible validation would require:

- continuous cell-bank telemetry;
- staged or reduced load;
- a conservative automatic cutoff below 60°C;
- measurement of fan-on versus fan-off temperature response;
- controls for fan failure and changing wind;
- inspection of why bank three runs hot.

My recommendation remains: do not treat the fan as authorization to run the original test. Use it only as a measured cooling variable in a controlled thermal qualification.
